  "enhanced_information": "Rheumatoid arthritis, Sjögren's syndrome, and systemic lupus erythematosus are systemic autoimmune diseases characterized by the immune system attacking the body's own tissues, leading to inflammation and tissue damage. They share common autoimmune mechanisms involving the production of autoantibodies against self-antigens. While Sjögren's syndrome is associated with chronic inflammation and autoantibodies, lupus erythematosus and rheumatoid arthritis also involve autoantibodies. Therefore, they are systemic autoimmune diseases rather than organospecific, and they are not primarily due to type I or type IV hypersensitivity reactions."
